<section class="section">
  <div class="shell about-narrative">
    <p class="section-index">The story so far</p>
    <div class="story-chapter"><span>2017</span><div><h2>I started Upstack Studio.</h2><p>For years, my team and I built custom web and mobile software with business owners. We sat close to the real decisions: what customers needed, what teams could adopt, where projects lost momentum and when software was—or was not—the answer.</p><p>That experience shaped how I think today. Technical capability matters. Commercial judgment matters more.</p></div></div>
    <figure class="wide-story-photo"><img src="/assets/images/adrian/upstack-team.webp" alt="Adrian Ching with the Upstack Studio team" loading="lazy" width="1130" height="1130"><figcaption>The Upstack Studio team.</figcaption></figure>
    <div class="story-chapter"><span>Then</span><div><h2>I chose to close the agency.</h2><p>Not because the work had no value, but because it was no longer the model I wanted to spend the next decade optimizing. I wanted to own the outcome, build assets that compound and place fewer, more ambitious bets.</p><p>There is no dramatic hero journey in that decision. It was simply the honest one.</p></div></div>
    <div class="story-chapter"><span>Now</span><div><h2>AI changed the economics of software.</h2><p>Building is becoming faster and cheaper. That shifts value toward problem selection, customer understanding, distribution and the judgment to know what should exist in the first place.</p><p>It also makes a founder’s job more interesting: smaller teams can create more, but they can also waste money faster. The advantage belongs to people who pair new capability with clear economic thinking.</p></div></div>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="section section-dark" id="what-im-building">
  <div class="shell about-build-grid">
    <div><p class="section-index">The work ahead</p><h2>Two ambitions.<br>One direction.</h2></div>
    <div class="build-ambition"><span>01</span><h3>Second Team</h3><p>My primary venture-scale company and the focus of most of my working time. I am building it for a future in which AI changes how companies discover and act on growth opportunities.</p><p>I’ll share the lessons, not sensitive or unreleased product IP.</p></div>
    <div class="build-ambition"><span>02</span><h3>A maker-led portfolio</h3><p>Over time, I’m building a portfolio of software products, digital assets, media and other high-margin businesses that I own and improve.</p><p>This website is the permanent home for that body of work.</p></div>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="section">
  <div class="shell values-grid">
    <div><p class="section-index">How I try to work</p><h2>Useful over impressive.</h2></div>
    <div class="values-list"><p><span>01</span>Start with the business, not the tool.</p><p><span>02</span>Say what is true, including when the answer is “don’t build it.”</p><p><span>03</span>Prefer ownership, focus and compounding over activity.</p><p><span>04</span>Share from experience, not from a borrowed playbook.</p></div>
  </div>
</section>

